  ('695733f9-f682-56a1-b821-00bffbccfd6b', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'exponents-scientific-notation'), null, 'hard',
   $q$In a science lab, Priya measures two salt samples. One has a mass of $2.5 \times 10^{-2}$ grams and the other has a mass of $7 \times 10^{-3}$ grams. What is their total mass, in grams?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$3.2 \\times 10^{-4}$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"adjusted-exponent-wrong-direction"},{"text":"$9.5 \\times 10^{-5}$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"added-exponents-when-adding"},{"text":"$3.2 \\times 10^{-2}$","is_correct":true},{"text":"$9.5 \\times 10^{-2}$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"added-coefficients-without-matching-exponents"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$3.2 \times 10^{-2}$$q$, $q$Write both masses with the same power of 10: $2.5 \times 10^{-2} = 25 \times 10^{-3}$. Then $25 + 7 = 32$, so the total is $32 \times 10^{-3} = 3.2 \times 10^{-2}$ grams. Check: 0.025 + 0.007 = 0.032. The choice $9.5 \times 10^{-2}$ adds 2.5 and 7 even though the powers of 10 do not match.$q$, 'draft', null)
